WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.770
Spain is in the middle of a political battle
that threatens to split the country.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:27.030
The region of Catalonia held a referendum
to decide if they wanted to become an independent

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:28.030
state.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.420
And therefore separate from Spain.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.890
The Spanish constitutional courts ruled the
referendum illegal and the central government

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.910
tried to stop it from happening.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.660
They arrested government officials who were
planning on holding the vote, confiscated

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.510
ballots, and closed polling stations.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:47.600
Undeterred the regional Catalan government
announced that voters could print their ballots

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.460
at home and vote at any open polling station.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.649
It didn’t take long for the situation to
escalate.

00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:59.370
Nearly 1000 people were injured in the violence.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.960
As for the results, 90 percent of participants
voted to secede.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.619
This would seem like almost unanimous approval,
but less than half of all eligible voters

00:01:08.619 --> 00:01:11.540
in Catalonia turned out to vote.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:17.829
Based on the results, the Catalan government
has declared independence from Spain unilaterally.

00:01:17.829 --> 00:01:23.040
In response the Spanish government is preparing
an unprecedented take over OF Catalonia’s

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.439
regional powers which will remove its leaders
from office.

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:29.429
So how did they get here?

00:01:29.429 --> 00:01:34.490
Spain is made up of 17 semi-autonomous regions,
each with their own identity.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:38.590
Some, like Catalonia, are distinct enough
that they have their own language, as well

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:42.049
as culture, cuisine and literature.

00:01:42.049 --> 00:01:44.850
Spain is a decentralized unified state.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:49.849
That means that autonomous regions like Catalonia,
are governed according to the national constitution

00:01:49.849 --> 00:01:53.349
as well as laws enacted independently by each
regional government.

00:01:53.349 --> 00:01:55.600
But it wasn’t always this way.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.740
From 1939 to 1975 Spain lived under a fascist
dictatorship.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:06.919
Francisco Franco eliminated democratic liberties,
freedom of the press and political opposition.

00:02:06.919 --> 00:02:13.310
He also crushed regional diversity around
the country to impose a single national identity.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:17.500
For Catalonia that meant suppressing the Catalan
language as well as local traditions.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.980
It was a big blow to the Catalan culture.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:27.400
When Franco died in 1975, Spain transitioned
into a democracy and the newly created constitution

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.540
expanded autonomy in the regions.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.480
they still use today.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.409
The constitution allowed Catalonia’s self
governance.

00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:39.069
It recognized Catalan as an official language
of Catalonia.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:43.849
And while specifically recognizing autonomy
in the regions that comprise Spain, it also

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:47.129
cemented that Spain was indivisible.

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:50.879
As Spain’s democracy strengthened, so did
Catalonia’s pride.

00:02:50.879 --> 00:02:57.572
We are Catalan, we feel Catalan we speak Catalan
and it’s another culture.

00:02:57.572 --> 00:03:02.060
Then, for the first time since the constitution
was created, Catalans tried to expand their

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.620
autonomy, The 2006 referendum called for many
things, including:

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:13.109
To create a new economic model for Catalonia
To define Catalonia as a nation

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:17.769
And to privilege the Catalan language over
Spanish

00:03:17.769 --> 00:03:23.849
After four years of legal battles the constitutional
court struck down some of the amendments.

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:28.790
When the constitutional ruling was announced,
one million Catalans went to the streets of

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:33.620
Barcelona, where they carried signs that read
“We are a nation.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.720
We decide."

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.269
There are economic motivations for independence
too.

00:03:39.269 --> 00:03:42.409
Catalonia is one of the most prosperous regions
in Spain.

00:03:42.409 --> 00:03:47.370
It takes up only 6 percent of the country,
but it accounts for one fifth of Spain’s

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:50.239
economic output.

00:03:50.239 --> 00:03:56.170
In 2016 nearly 21 percent of Spain’s tax
revenue came from Catalonia.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:04:04.249
We pay a lot of taxes, they take them away
and they don’t return it in better infrastructure

00:04:04.249 --> 00:04:09.609
or better living conditions.

00:04:09.609 --> 00:04:14.239
Pro-independence Catalans believe Catalonia
pays too much money into the central government

00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:16.600
compared to what it gets in return.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:21.670
Catalonia, like Madrid and other richer regions,
support the poorer parts of Spain.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:29.360
This sentiment grew during the European economic
crisis.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.030
when a wave of unemployment hit Spain.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:36.741
But despite growing support, the majority
of Catalans don’t want independence.

00:04:36.741 --> 00:04:38.500
And while support for independence has been
close to 50 percent, the majority of Catalans

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.500
do not want to leave Spain.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:50.340
Low income families are less likely to support
independence than those who are well off.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.920
As are Catalans who live in cities compared
to those who live in rural areas.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:58.900
Which brings us back to today.

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:01.560
Banks and multinationals have started to move
their headquarters out of Barcelona to other

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.030
Spanish regions.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:08.310
Catalan separatists have turned to Europe
for support, but most European Union leaders

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:10.130
have sided with the central government.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:15.480
They have made it clear that an independent
Catalonia would have to apply for EU membership,

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.760
a process that would take years.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.920
And while the fight on Catalan independence
will eventually be settled, it may take a

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.350
long time for Catalonia and Spain to return
to peaceful coexistence.

